03:38:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see where you are coming from—if you survive something, it’s easy to look back and say, "Well, I didn't die, so it must not have been that bad!" But equating "survivable" with "safe" misses what food is actually supposed to do. Think about it in a modern context: If you eat raw chicken that’s been sitting in the sun for two days, you probably won't die. You’ll just get severe food poisoning, vomit for 24 hours, and eventually recover. But no one would ever call eating rotting raw chicken "safe." Here is why, both scientifically and practically, there is a massive middle ground between "perfectly safe" and "instantly lethal": **1. The Definition of Poison** In science and medicine, "poisonous" or "toxic" does not mean "lethal." A poison is simply any substance that causes physiological harm or illness when absorbed by the body. * **Poison Ivy** is a great example. If you eat it, it will cause severe, agonizing blistering in your throat and stomach. It almost certainly won't kill you, but it is undeniably poisonous and absolutely unsafe to eat. **2. The Food Equation (Calories In vs. Calories Out)** For early humans, the entire point of eating was to gain energy. If a plant gives you a stomachache and makes you vomit, it has failed its basic function as food. If you eat a handful of toxic berries and gain 50 calories, but then you spend the night vomiting and sweating, you might lose 300 calories and a quart of water. If you keep eating that "survivable" plant, you will literally starve and dehydrate to death. It is unsafe because it takes away more life-sustaining energy than it gives. **3. Damage to Organs** Many things that don't kill you immediately cause permanent damage. Drinking a small amount of antifreeze or rubbing alcohol won't necessarily kill a person, but it can cause permanent blindness or destroy your liver. Early humans learned that certain plants caused long-term pain, jaundice, or weakness. Surviving the experience didn't make the plant safe; it just meant they had to live with the damage. **4. The Threshold of "Edible"** When we say a plant or animal is "edible," we don't just mean "you can swallow it and survive." We mean it is **nourishing and harmless**. If a plant requires your immune system and liver to work overtime just to keep you from dying, your body is fighting off a poison. To our ancestors, "safe" meant a food provided energy without a biological penalty. If there was a penalty—like cramps, vomiting, or hallucinations—that item was firmly placed in the "poison" category, because in the harsh reality of survival, a sick human was a dead human.
