03:39:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You make a really great point, and honestly, your parents and teachers were doing exactly what they were supposed to do! That is exactly how almost all of us were taught about nature growing up. When you think about it from a parent's or teacher's perspective, it makes perfect sense why they use that exact phrasing. Here is why we all grew up hearing "don't eat that, it'll kill you," and how it bridges the gap between everyday life and the science of survival: **1. The Ultimate Deterrent for Kids** Kids don't really do well with nuance, and they are naturally curious. If a parent tells a five-year-old, "Don't eat that wild berry, it will give you severe diarrhea and stomach cramps," a kid might think, *Well, I like berries, and I've had a tummy ache before. Maybe it's worth it.* But if an adult says, "Don't eat that, it will kill you!" the kid drops the berry immediately. Adults use black-and-white, life-or-death language because it is the absolute best way to keep a child safe. It leaves no room for a kid to experiment. **2. The "Better Safe Than Sorry" Shortcut** Because we are taught this as kids, "poisonous = deadly" becomes the working definition for most of us in everyday conversation. And for modern life, that definition works perfectly! If you are hiking in the woods today, you don't need to know the exact medical difference between a mushroom that causes liver failure and a mushroom that just makes you throw up for three days. You just need to know to leave it alone. "It'll kill you" is a cultural shortcut that means "this is not food, do not put it in your mouth under any circumstances." **3. You Benefited from Ancient Generational Knowledge** The really cool thing about your parents and teachers warning you is that they were participating in the exact same survival tactic early humans used! Thousands of years ago, our ancestors did the messy, painful work of figuring out exactly *what* those plants did to the human body. Once they figured out a plant was harmful—whether it killed someone or just made them violently ill—they passed that rule down to their kids to protect them. Your parents didn't need to know the specific chemical makeup of a toxic plant; they just knew the ancient rule they were taught: *Don't eat it.* They gave you the "CliffsNotes" version of human survival so you wouldn't have to find out the hard way!
