03:40:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are absolutely right that when foraging goes wrong in the news, it is almost always a tragedy. We’ve all seen those heartbreaking headlines about a family eating wild mushrooms and ending up in the ICU or worse. When you see those stories, it completely validates the idea that "poisonous" means "lethal." But what you are seeing is actually a classic case of how the media shapes our perception of danger. Here is why you only hear about the deaths, even though the reality of toxic plants is much broader: **1. The News Only Reports the Extremes** Think about how the news works: it reports on the extraordinary, not the everyday. If someone gets in a horrific, fatal car crash on the highway, it makes the evening news. If someone gets into a minor fender-bender in a grocery store parking lot, no one writes an article about it. The same applies to eating wild plants. If a hiker eats a handful of toxic berries, spends the night vomiting in their tent, and hikes out the next morning feeling embarrassed and dehydrated, they don't call the local news station. The media only reports on the absolute worst-case scenarios because that is what makes a compelling (and tragic) story. **2. The Reality of Poison Control Centers** If you look at the actual data from Poison Control centers, the picture looks very different from the evening news. Every single year, thousands of people—mostly toddlers who grab things in the yard—eat toxic wild berries, leaves, and mushrooms. In the vast majority of these cases, the person does not die. The Poison Control operator usually tells the panicked parents to monitor the child for vomiting, diarrhea, or a rash, and maybe take them to the pediatrician just in case. There are tens of thousands of these "middle ground" poisonings every year, but because everyone survives, they never make the news. **3. The "Death Cap" Factor** That being said, the stories you hear on the news are very real, and they usually involve a specific culprit: mushrooms. Mushrooms are notoriously tricky. While many toxic plants taste incredibly bitter to warn you away, some of the most lethal mushrooms in the world—like the "Death Cap" (*Amanita phalloides*) or the "Destroying Angel"—reportedly taste quite good. Furthermore, their toxins don't make you sick right away; they quietly destroy your liver over a few days. When adults go foraging and make a fatal mistake, it is very often with these specific, highly lethal mushrooms. Because these specific fungi *do* act exactly like the black-and-white "it will kill you" definition, they dominate the headlines. **The Bottom Line** You aren't wrong for associating poisonous plants with death, because the lethal ones are the only ones we ever hear about! The news reinforces the exact same lesson your parents taught you. But behind those rare, tragic headlines are thousands of everyday incidents where people eat the wrong plant, get violently sick, and survive—just like our ancestors did.
